<http://purl.org/semsur/Type-Constrained_Representation_Learning_in_Knowledge_Graphs> a [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:allValuesFrom ns1:Person ;
            owl:onProperty ns1:author ],
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:allValuesFrom ns1:Publication ;
            owl:onProperty ns1:cite ],
        <http://purl.org/semsur/RegularPaper>,
        <http://purl.org/semsur/SingleAuthorPublication>,
        ns1:Article,
        ns1:Publication,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:creator <http://purl.org/semsur/Denis_Krompaß>,
        <http://purl.org/semsur/Stephan_Baier>,
        <http://purl.org/semsur/Volker_Tresp> ;
    dcterms:abstract "Large knowledge graphs increasingly add value to various\\napplications that require machines to recognize and understand queries\\nand their semantics, as in search or question answering systems. La-\\ntent variable models have increasingly gained attention for the statistical\\nmodeling of knowledge graphs, showing promising results in tasks related\\nto knowledge graph completion and cleaning. Besides storing facts about\\nthe world, schema-based knowledge graphs are backed by rich semantic\\ndescriptions of entities and relation-types that allow machines to un-\\nderstand the notion of things and their semantic relationships. In this\\nwork, we study how type-constraints can generally support the statistical\\nmodeling with latent variable models. More precisely, we integrated prior\\nknowledge in form of type-constraints in various state of the art latent\\nvariable approaches. Our experimental results show that prior knowledge\\non relation-types significantly improves these models up to 77% in link-\\nprediction tasks. The achieved improvements are especially prominent\\nwhen a low model complexity is enforced, a crucial requirement when\\nthese models are applied to very large datasets. Unfortunately, type-\\nconstraints are neither always available nor always complete e.g., they\\ncan become fuzzy when entities lack proper typing. We show that in\\nthese cases, it can be beneficial to apply a local closed-world assumption\\nthat approximates the semantics of relation-types based on observations\\nmade in the data." ;
    dcterms:description "Large knowledge graphs increasingly add value to various\\napplications that require machines to recognize and understand queries\\nand their semantics, as in search or question answering systems. La-\\ntent variable models have increasingly gained attention for the statistical\\nmodeling of knowledge graphs, showing promising results in tasks related\\nto knowledge graph completion and cleaning. Besides storing facts about\\nthe world, schema-based knowledge graphs are backed by rich semantic\\ndescriptions of entities and relation-types that allow machines to un-\\nderstand the notion of things and their semantic relationships. In this\\nwork, we study how type-constraints can generally support the statistical\\nmodeling with latent variable models. More precisely, we integrated prior\\nknowledge in form of type-constraints in various state of the art latent\\nvariable approaches. Our experimental results show that prior knowledge\\non relation-types significantly improves these models up to 77% in link-\\nprediction tasks. The achieved improvements are especially prominent\\nwhen a low model complexity is enforced, a crucial requirement when\\nthese models are applied to very large datasets. Unfortunately, type-\\nconstraints are neither always available nor always complete e.g., they\\ncan become fuzzy when entities lack proper typing. We show that in\\nthese cases, it can be beneficial to apply a local closed-world assumption\\nthat approximates the semantics of relation-types based on observations\\nmade in the data." .

<http://purl.org/semsur/Type_Inference_on_Noisy_RDF_Data> a [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:allValuesFrom ns1:Person ;
            owl:onProperty ns1:author ],
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:allValuesFrom ns1:Publication ;
            owl:onProperty ns1:cite ],
        <http://purl.org/semsur/RegularPaper>,
        <http://purl.org/semsur/SingleAuthorPublication>,
        ns1:Article,
        ns1:Publication,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:creator <http://purl.org/semsur/Christian_Bizer>,
        <http://purl.org/semsur/Heiko_Paulheim> ;
    dcterms:abstract " Type information is very valuable in knowledge bases. However, most large open knowledge bases are incomplete with respect to type information, and, at the same time, contain noisy and incorrect data. That makes classic type inference by reasoning difficult. In this paper, we propose the heuristic link-based type inference mechanism SD- Type, which can handle noisy and incorrect data. Instead of leveraging T-box information from the schema, SDType takes the actual use of a schema into account and thus is also robust to misused schema elements." ;
    dcterms:description " Type information is very valuable in knowledge bases. However, most large open knowledge bases are incomplete with respect to type information, and, at the same time, contain noisy and incorrect data. That makes classic type inference by reasoning difficult. In this paper, we propose the heuristic link-based type inference mechanism SD- Type, which can handle noisy and incorrect data. Instead of leveraging T-box information from the schema, SDType takes the actual use of a schema into account and thus is also robust to misused schema elements." ;
    ns1:keywords "Type Inference, Noisy Data, Link-based Classification" .

<http://purl.org/semsur/Type_Prediction_for_Efficient_Coreference_Resolution_in_Heterogeneous_Semantic_Graphs> a [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:allValuesFrom ns1:Person ;
            owl:onProperty ns1:author ],
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:allValuesFrom ns1:Publication ;
            owl:onProperty ns1:cite ],
        <http://purl.org/semsur/RegularPaper>,
        <http://purl.org/semsur/SingleAuthorPublication>,
        ns1:Article,
        ns1:Publication,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dc:creator <http://purl.org/semsur/Jennifer_Sleeman>,
        <http://purl.org/semsur/Tim_Finin> ;
    ns1:keywords "We describe an approach for performing entity type recognition in heterogeneous semantic graphs in order to reduce the computational cost of performing coreference resolution. Our research specifically addresses the problem of working with semi-structured text that\\nuses ontologies that are not informative or not known. This problem is similar to coreference resolution in unstructured text, where entities and their types are identified using contextual information and linguistic-based analysis. Semantic graphs are semi-structured with very\\nlittle contextual information and trivial grammars that do not convey additional information. In the absence of known ontologies, performing coreference resolution can be challenging. Our work uses a supervised machine learning algorithm and entity type dictionaries to map attributes to a common attribute space. We evaluated the approach in experiments using data from Wikipedia, Freebase and Arnetminer." .

<http://purl.org/semsur/Usability> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/semsur/EvaluationDimensions> .

<http://purl.org/semsur/Vojtach_Svaitek> a dcterms:Agent,
        <http://schema.org/Person>,
        <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/pim/contact#Person>,
        owl:NamedIndividual,
        <http://www.w3.org/2003/01/geo/wgs84_pos#SpatialThing>,
        foaf:Agent,
        foaf:Person .

<http://purl.org/semsur/WoDQA_Conclusion> a <http://purl.org/orb/1.0/Conclusion>,
        <http://purl.org/spar/deo/Conclusion>,
        <http://purl.org/spar/deo/DiscourseElement>,
        <http://salt.semanticauthoring.org/ontologies/sro#Conclusion>,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    doap:description "In this paper, we have introduced a query federation engine calledWoDQA that discovers related datasets in a VOID\\nstore for a query and distributes the query over these datasets. The novelty of our approach is exhaustive dataset selection\\nmechanism which includes analysis of triple pattern relations and links between datasets besides analyzing datasets for\\neach triple pattern. WoDQA focuses on discovering relevant datasets and eliminating irrelevant ones using a rule-based\\napproach introduced in this paper. Our approach requires VOID descriptions which include a SPARQL endpoint to\\nquery the dataset, reflect actual content of the dataset completely and accurately, and include linksets between datasets\\nto select datasets effectively. WoDQA allows users to construct raw queries without the need to know how query will\\ndivide into sub-queries and where sub-queries are executed. Query results are complete under the assumption of available,\\naccurate and complete VOID descriptions of datasets. The initial version of WoDQA which is introduced in this\\npaper has some disadvantages arising from query federation approach which WoDQA builds upon. As mentioned previously,\\nfollow-your-nose has some problems such as missing results and large document retrieval. Similar problems may\\noccur for query federation. Firstly, to find complete resultsto queries, it is required that metadata of all datasets must\\nbe well-defined and accurate. But, to provide such an accurate dataset metadata an automated mechanism which\\ncontinuously updates the metadata is required. However, even there would be a tool which implements this requirement,\\nproviding accurate dataset metadata via such a tool is the responsibility of dataset publishers.\\nAnother problems of query federation are high latency and low selectivity of datasets which are similar to retrieval\\nof large documents in follow-your-nose. Query optimization can be a solution for these problems of query federation.\\nGrouping triple patterns to filter more triples on an endpoint can prevent high latency (required processing time)\\nand changing query evaluation order according to dataset selectivity statistics can prevent retrieving large result sets.\\nTo make WoDQA functioning in the wild, optimization step of query federation is required to be implemented. We plan\\nto incorporate triple pattern selectivity into query reorganization using VOID properties about statistics.\\nOn the other hand, we could not make an evaluation of our approach in this paper, since VOID documents in current\\nVOID stores are not well-defined. Since SPARQL endpoint definitions, linkset descriptions or vocabularies are missing\\nin most of VOID documents, we could not find a chance to execute comprehensive scenarios." .

<http://purl.org/semsur/WoDQA_evaluation> a <http://purl.org/spar/deo/Evaluation>,
        owl:NamedIndividual .

<http://purl.org/semsur/WolframWoay> a dcterms:Agent,
